Pages = {2487-2510},
Month = {DEC},
Abstract = {Academic harassment, which is the abuse of power in higher education, is
   a global concern in science academia. It hinders the careers of young
   researchers, particularly graduate students, and creates a toxic
   environment that fosters misconduct and turnover. Consequently,
   scientific progress is slowed and national scientific integrity is
   undermined. The specific psychological tendencies of harassers could be
   a potential antecedent of harassment. However, as the full range of
   these tendencies has not been explored, we developed and validated the
   Academic Harassment Tendency Scale for Science Academia (AHTS-SA). The
   initial AHTS-SA was developed based on a preliminary survey through item
   generation, content validation, and examination of the score
   distribution. The main survey consisted of two phases. In Phase 1, 267
   faculty members (245 men, 22 women) and 233 graduate students (131 men,
   101 women, and 1 other) in science academia completed the initial
   AHTS-SA along with other harassment-related scales. As a result,
   exploratory factor analysis revealed three factors: self-centered
   entitlement, ageism, and emotional dysregulation. In Phase 2, the
   initial AHTS-SA was readministered 1 month later to all Phase 1
   participants, and the responses from 60 participants were analyzed to
   examine the test-retest reliability. Reliability was confirmed through
   internal consistency and test-retest reliability. Convergent and
   concurrent validities were also confirmed by correlating the AHTS-SA
   with the Perception of Academic Harassment and Gender Harassment Scales
   developed in Japan. The AHTS-SA is a potential tool for quality
   assurance within organizations and for individual researchers to become
   aware of their emotions and biases.},
